WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.673
Our post office has outgrown its one clerk, so it hires a team.

2
00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:10.078
Each clerk keeps a row of boxes in the wall, for good.

3
00:00:10.078 --> 00:00:14.240
And every letter from those boxes passes along a line of stations.

4
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:17.284
The counter in this lesson is the price desk.

5
00:00:17.284 --> 00:00:23.608
A customer sends a line describing a letter or a parcel, and the desk answers with a line of its own.

6
00:00:23.608 --> 00:00:27.991
Our demo desk is simpler still: it shouts every line back in capitals.

7
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.749
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:31.749 --> 00:00:36.086
In Netty, you build that line with addLast, one handler after another.

9
00:00:36.086 --> 00:00:38.130
So is that the order they run in?

10
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.599
And why does a reply sometimes skip a handler you added?

11
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:49.332
Let's map the line onto Netty, a Java library for network servers.

12
00:00:49.332 --> 00:00:54.632
Each connection is a channel, and each channel has a pipeline: a line of handlers.

13
00:00:54.632 --> 00:01:00.255
A handler is one station, a small object that reacts to what happens on the connection.

14
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:01.936
The line has two ends.

15
00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:05.542
The head sits next to the socket, the box in the wall.

16
00:01:05.542 --> 00:01:07.145
The tail is the far end.

17
00:01:07.145 --> 00:01:13.891
And each handler gets a ctx, its own place on the line, with ways to pass things to the next station.

18
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.823
Our desk has four stations.

19
00:01:15.823 --> 00:01:19.907
LineBasedFrameDecoder cuts the stream of bytes into whole lines.

20
00:01:19.907 --> 00:01:24.119
StringDecoder reads the handwriting: it turns bytes into a String.

21
00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:26.607
The business handler writes the answer.

22
00:01:26.607 --> 00:01:31.776
And StringEncoder is the packing station, which turns the answer back into bytes.

23
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.665
Letters coming in are inbound events.

24
00:01:34.665 --> 00:01:40.359
They start at the head and travel toward the tail: framing, then decoding, then your handler.

25
00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:43.727
So your handler only ever sees whole lines, as Strings.

26
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:45.223
Replies are outbound.

27
00:01:45.223 --> 00:01:49.337
A write starts where you call it, and travels back toward the head.

28
00:01:49.337 --> 00:01:54.065
On the way, it passes the packing station, so it reaches the socket as bytes.

29
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:58.570
So the order of addLast is the order inbound events see the handlers.

30
00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.253
Writes see them in reverse.

31
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:01.862
Let's watch it.

32
00:02:01.862 --> 00:02:08.693
The lesson's PipelineDemo puts taps between the stations, small handlers that print whatever passes by.

33
00:02:08.693 --> 00:02:12.541
Then it feeds in two lines, cut into three awkward pieces.

34
00:02:12.541 --> 00:02:14.995
What comes out after the first piece?

35
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:19.104
Nothing at all.

36
00:02:19.104 --> 00:02:23.919
The framing station kept the three bytes, and waited for the end of the line.

37
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:26.118
The second piece finishes hello.

38
00:02:26.118 --> 00:02:29.964
The tap after framing sees a ByteBuf, Netty's buffer of bytes.

39
00:02:29.964 --> 00:02:32.135
The business handler sees a String.

40
00:02:32.135 --> 00:02:36.043
And HELLO, with its newline, goes out to the wire as a ByteBuf.

41
00:02:36.043 --> 00:02:38.524
The third piece does the same for world.

42
00:02:38.733 --> 00:02:42.443
The framing station also has a limit: 64 bytes a line.

43
00:02:42.443 --> 00:02:46.771
Picture a customer who talks and talks, and never says newline.

44
00:02:46.771 --> 00:02:50.550
Without a limit, the station keeps those bytes forever.

45
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:55.153
With it, a 100-byte line fails at once, with TooLongFrameException.

46
00:02:55.153 --> 00:02:58.244
Some customers simply need to be interrupted.

47
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:03.163
Now let's test the easy model, that handlers just run in addLast order.

48
00:03:03.163 --> 00:03:06.673
Move the outbound tap so it comes right after the encoder.

49
00:03:06.673 --> 00:03:10.728
If that model were right, the tap would still print the same thing.

50
00:03:10.728 --> 00:03:12.120
What does it print now?

51
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.152
String, HELLO.

52
00:03:16.152 --> 00:03:23.458
The write leaves the business handler and travels toward the head, so now it reaches the tap before the packing station.

53
00:03:23.458 --> 00:03:26.563
A handler's place on the line decides what it sees.

54
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:31.200
One more detail decides which stations a write passes.

55
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:37.100
ctx.writeAndFlush starts at the current handler, and skips everything after it.

56
00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:43.224
ctx.channel().writeAndFlush starts at the tail, and passes every outbound station.

57
00:03:43.224 --> 00:03:48.079
Inside a handler, use ctx, unless you really want the whole line.

58
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.290
Here the post office picture breaks.

59
00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.250
On a real sorting line, every station has its own worker, all working at once.

60
00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:03.055
In Netty, one clerk carries each letter through every station, start to finish, before picking up the next.

61
00:04:03.055 --> 00:04:04.391
One clerk, four hats.

62
00:04:04.391 --> 00:04:10.751
So one slow station holds up the whole row of boxes, and that's the golden rule of the next section.

63
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:14.663
And notice, PipelineDemo has no socket at all.

64
00:04:14.663 --> 00:04:18.441
It runs on an EmbeddedChannel, a channel you feed by hand.

65
00:04:18.441 --> 00:04:25.281
writeInbound pushes bytes in at the head, and readOutbound collects what reaches the head on the way out.

66
00:04:25.281 --> 00:04:27.821
It's the fastest way to test a station.

67
00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:36.399
Here's the desk's line in code, in Java and in Kotlin: two decoders, the encoder, and the desk, in addLast order.

68
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.549
So, do handlers run in addLast order?

69
00:04:39.549 --> 00:04:41.495
Inbound, yes: from head to tail.

70
00:04:41.495 --> 00:04:45.568
Writes go the other way, from where you start them toward the head.

71
00:04:45.568 --> 00:04:49.337
Picture the line, and you can predict what every station sees.

72
00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:50.381
Quick check.

73
00:04:50.381 --> 00:04:55.197
You add a logging station at the very end, after your business handler.

74
00:04:55.197 --> 00:04:58.724
The business handler replies with ctx.writeAndFlush.

75
00:04:58.724 --> 00:05:01.369
Does the logging station see the reply?

76
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:04.743
No.

77
00:05:04.743 --> 00:05:11.043
That write starts at the business handler and travels toward the head, so a station after it never sees it.

78
00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:14.635
Put the logger nearer the head, or write through the channel.
